      en: "Norrköping is a significant industrial city in the Östergötland province, located at the mouth of the Motala ström river in the Bråviken bay of the Baltic Sea. The city flourished during the Industrial Revolution when the river's hydropower fueled numerous textile mills, earning it the nickname \"The Manchester of Sweden.\" The heart of the city is the Industrilandskapet (Industrial Landscape), a unique area of restored brick factories along the rushing water, now housing museums, university buildings, and innovative companies. Particularly noteworthy is the Arbetets museum (Museum of Work), located in a distinctive heptagonal building called \"The Iron\" situated directly in the river. Norrköping is also famous for its yellow trams, which have been part of the cityscape since 1904 and represent one of the few surviving tram networks in Sweden. The city serves as the headquarters for important national institutions, such as the Swedish Meteorological and Hydrological Institute (SMHI). Culturally, Norrköping offers a rich program through the Louis De Geer concert hall and various art galleries, blending historical tradition with modern urban life.",
